  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough that has helped cause the active weather \nacross west central Texas today will start to shift eastward \nFriday evening into Saturday, leaving behind shortwave ridging \nacross Texas. Cold overnight low temperatures and cooler afternoon\nhighs will remain in the wake of the cold front tracking through \nthe region tonight. A much cooler and more stable airmass will \nsettle in across the region for the weekend. Overnight lows Friday\nnight under generally clear skies and light to slightly breezy \nwinds can allow for ideal radiational cooling conditions early \nSaturday morning as well as Sunday morning. As a result, low \ntemperatures both mornings could be quite cool as they look to \ndrop into the upper 30s to mid 40s. Apparent temperatures can drop\ninto the low 30s to low 40s, which brings us close to freezing in\nsome low- lying areas. This would be the coldest temperatures we \nhave experienced since mid-April!\n\nThe strong area of high pressure will move eastward by Sunday \nafternoon and allow for southerly flow at the surface while an \nupper-level shortwave passes to the north. High temperatures \nshould climb back into the 70s on Sunday and Monday and into the \n80s on Tuesday. The upper-level pattern could become more active \nfor the middle part of next week as global models are in fairly \ngood agreement of an upper-level low digging into southern \nCalifornia by Tuesday night. This would place much of west Texas \nunder southwest flow aloft, similar to what occurred earlier this \nweek. Differences in timing and moisture amounts still exist on \nthis feature so for now, low (20-30%) chances for rain are in the \nforecast for Wednesday.",
